WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.300
Hey this is Presh Talwalker. I received an email that I wanted to share with you.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:09.340
Wolfgang came up with a false proof that 2 equals 0

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:16.440
When he showed it to his class no one could figure out where the mistake was not even his teacher

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:24.180
So I wanted to share a version of that proof with you and ask. Can you figure out where the mistake is?

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:29.100
We start out with two equals one plus one

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:34.540
one is also equal to the square root of one,

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.620
and one is also equal to negative 1 times negative one

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:43.800
We now take the square root of each term

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:49.560
the Square root of negative 1 is equal to the imaginary number i.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:58.060
We have i times i which equals i squared and i squared is equal to negative 1.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:05.240
In the end we have 1 plus negative 1 which equals 0 and all of this is equal to 2

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:09.120
So we have proven that 2 is equal to 0?

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.960
Clearly this is an absurd conclusion. So where is the mistake in this proof?

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:25.060
give it a moment of thought before watching the rest of the video where I explain where the mistake comes from

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:37.820
So when wolfgang sent me this email. I immediately spotted the mistake because it is a common fallacy

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:45.060
the problem comes when you take the square root of a product of negative terms

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:49.660
It's ok to take the square root of negative 1 times negative 1

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:54.960
What you cannot do is then take the square root of each term.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:03.900
There is something called the product rule of square roots if you have the square root of a times b

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:07.740
That is equal to the square root of a times the square root of b

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:13.120
But it's generally only true when both terms are greater than or equal to 0

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.399
it doesn't work in this case when both

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:23.190
Terms are negative 1. you can see that's the mistake all of the other steps in the proof are valid

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.790
So in math class you sometimes learn these properties

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:27.840
And they come with these

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:34.080
Conditions like  oh this is only true if both terms are greater than or equal to 0. you can see why these terms

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:41.480
absolutely matter you should remember the conditions of these properties because otherwise you can get to absurd conclusions

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:43.380
like in this Case 2 equals 0

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:46.350
So did you figure it out?

